@@ -51,3 +54,31 @@ on Linux x86 and ARM before their CI failure is considered resolved.
 The benchmark build also exposed stale `jmhRuntimeClasspath` and benchmark `runtimeClasspath`
 dependency locks. Regenerating them restores the JMH artifact build; CI now builds it on every
 PR. Full build, portability CI, and released dependency-chain validation remain required.
+
+## Dedicated-host follow-up
+
+The first isolated ABBA experiment (`isolated/`) passed all twelve correctness probes and the
+allocation checks on x86 and ARM. However, the ARM large prompt batch measured 1.07079 times
+baseline latency, exceeding the declared 1.05 investigation threshold. This is an unresolved
+performance regression; the candidate is not accepted for release. Both hosts are terminated.
+
+The next candidate replaces cyclic `rearrange` calls with two-vector `slice` calls in the same
+fixed reduction tree. This preserves the lane values and addition order while giving the JIT a
+contiguous rotation operation. Whether it improves the generated code is a hypothesis pending
+paired measurements; no architecture-specific routing or JVM inlining flags are introduced.
+The shared reduction helper and its constants return to their original implementation/visibility.
+
+The cyclic-slice experiment is also rejected for release. Its complete same-host ABBA results
+(`slice-experiment/isolated/`) show x86 mid-sized matvec at 1.10488 times baseline latency and ARM
+large prompt batches at 1.08721. All cold/warm and allocation checks passed, but those do not
+override the measured regressions. Both experiment hosts are verified terminated.
+
+The shortened large-shape probes remain sensitive: the original baseline fails the 256-bit tall
+case on matvec iteration 0 and the parallel case on batched iteration 2. The 64-bit tall case
+passes on the baseline, as expected for a two-lane sum; no claim is made that every width fails.
+Logs are retained in `slice-experiment/baseline-short-probes.json` and its referenced commands.
+
+`reduction-pilot/` compares the original fixed tree with a scalar final pair, a narrowed final
+vector, and explicit scalar lane sums. These are source-only hypotheses, all preserving the same
+addition tree. Candidate validity checks precede the paired benchmark; no variant is a release
+candidate until the complete relevant shape matrix and real-model tests pass.
